  "query_preview": "..\nToday through Saturday: A ridge firmly in place over the western US \nis bringing yet another hot day to the Inland Northwest. High \ntemperatures today will be in the mid to upper 90s with triple digit \ntemperatures in the deeper Basin and Lewiston areas. Widespread \nmoderate HeatRisk remains over the forecast area, so all those \nsensitive to heat should continue being cautious and have access to \nadequate cooling and hydration. A strong thermal trough will result \nin a breezy evening as the east-west pressure gradient across the \nCascades tightens this afternoon. Areas that will see the highest \nwind gusts (20 to 25 mph) are the Columbia Basin, Waterville \nPlateau, and Okanogan Valley areas. As a result, a Red Flag Warning \nhas been issued for these areas through late tonight due to \nthese wind gusts combined with high temperatures and relative \nhumidity values 10 to 20 percent. Elsewhere, expect wind gusts \n10 to 15 mph.\n\nAs Saturday nears, a jet streak will strengthen over the area, with \n500 mb flow nearing 50 to 60 knots. Additionally, a low pressure \nsystem off the coast will usher in a dry cold front Saturday \nmorning. This cold air advection combined with steep low level lapse \nrates will result in 30 to 40 kt winds at 700 mb mixing down to the \nsurface. The drier air moving in will keep relative humidities \nacross the Inland Northwest confined to 10 to 25 percent. Thus, a \nwidespread Red Flag Warning has been issued from 11am to 10pm \nSaturday. These fire weather conditions could result in rapid fire \nspread with any new or ongoing fires. Additionally, with wind gusts \nof this magnitude, there is a risk for blowing objects, damage to \nsmall trees, and localized power outages. High temperatures will \ndecrease by about 5 to 10 degrees between today and tomorrow, and \nlow temperatures will decrease by about 5 degrees. \n\nSunday onward: A series of low pressure systems will keep heights \nlower off the PNW coast, allowing temperatures to fall into the mid \nto high 80s for much of the area Sunday through Wednesday. These \nvalues are right around normal for this time of year. Onshore flow \nand the thermal trough will continue to bring breezy afternoons to \ncentral and eastern Washington. Dry conditions will continue through \nthis time. By Thursday and into the beginning of next weekend, the \nlow pressure off the PNW coast will weaken and a broad ridge forms \nover much of the western US. Long term and cluster guidance both \nfavor this ridge strengthening into next weekend. This will bring a \nreturn of minimum relative humidity values in the teens, as well as \nkeep conditions dry with little to no rainfall expected across the \nInland Northwest through next weekend. The CPC reflects this, with \nincreased chances for above normal temperatures and below normal \nprecipitation. /AS",
